A||||12||Rod101||Please help - Fiero won't start - A 2 year old mystery
Z||000000||Rod101||07-31-99||12:19 AM||||I have a 2 years old 87 coupe Fiero that is not able to start for 2 years. 4 cylinder, 99000 miles.<p>The History (then):<p>A friend of mine bought the car in 1997 and 3 months after that the car just stops running. When he turn the keys, nothing happens) He took it to a mechanic and after working on it for more than 1 month, he still not able to get it to start at all. The mechanic final words are '...the female wire harness need to be replaced...', '... spark plug won't spark..'. That is AFTER about $2000 has been used for parts and labor. Some parts replaced includes PCV valve, Oxy sensor, ECM, exhaust manifold, starter etc.<p>So the car was left to rot at a parking lot until...<p>NOW:<p>I decided to take on the challenge to repair the car... in exchange for the title ;) I brought the car to another mechanic. They towed it in the morning and that same afternoon diagnose that the battery is dead (obviously) and oil need to be change (too acidic after 2 years they said). They replace the battery and oil and turn the ignition key and ... it starts! The mechanic actually took the car out for spin and says that it works ok except the temperature guage is not working!<p>Okay... Am I lucky or what? I asked the mechanic to check the gauge and fix it. Then it happens. Today he called back and told me there is problem. The spark plug will not fire and fuel doesn't pressurized. The mystery has started again.<p>WHAT can be the cause of it? After reading some post and some 'service manuals'. It appears to me that the ignition module should be replaced right? Unlikely parts of the emissions control system failed since so many of its parts are brand new and there is no engine error code.<p>I would appreciate and help or insight from anyone to solve this mystery. Thank you.
Z||000001||lowCG||07-31-99||01:11 AM||||Sounds like the problem is somehow electrical.You need to follow the "flow of power" to,and from the components that make the car run.A blown up copy of a schematic(wiring diagram) is sometimes helpful with this.<br>Good luck.
Z||000002||Kage86GT4SP||07-31-99||01:45 AM||||My OTHER car, as they say, is an 86 Trans Am... (383/TPI/700R4) and I had a similar problem.<p>Nothing worked, and I couldn't even pull the codes from the service engine soon light... it wouldn't come on at all.<p>Eventually, I traced it to a fuse in the engine compartment that had to do with the ECM (main computer)<p>When I replaced the fuse, everything started working.  <p>So, the next question is this: does the SES light come on at all?  Can you pull the codes?  If you can't, I'd look for a blown fuse.<p>Kage
Z||000003||Rod101||07-31-99||02:36 AM||||Thanks for all your suggestions:<p>LowCG: Can you tell me where can I get the detail wiring diagrams from? Those diagrams that I found in generic service manual looks more like flow diagrams.<p>Kage: You are refering to those fuse at the engine area right? Let me try it the first thing next week.<p>Rod<br>
Z||000004||Kage86GT4SP||07-31-99||11:58 AM||||Yup, in the engine compartment.  On my TA, it was in the vicinity of the battery, but I haven't looked in my F.  I'll check on it today, and see if I can locate one.<p>K.
Z||000005||four50four||07-31-99||03:39 PM||||heres the deal, my 400 000+km prelude had the same problem when i ran out of gas one day. so i "hot wired' the fuel pump (don't know if it was related, but it didn't work.) after much "diagnostics", i hooked up a wire from the positve on the battery to the positive on the coil. it works. just a thought
Z||000006||theogre||08-01-99||01:57 PM||||I say this allot, but on GM cars........ It's a fact of life.<p>Check the 2 small leads from the battery, and the fuse links. GM runs the whole car off the thin + wire from the battery. The thick lead goes only to the starter. Replace the battery cables if they even look bad.<p>Check all other grounds as well. There are a number of them. Make sure they are all good. Reterminate all flaky looking ends, or replace wires as required.
Z||000007||Racer 1||08-02-99||12:13 PM||||Rod maybe your mechanic is scamming ya.If it ran fine then he fixed the temp gauge then it didnt run hmmmm maybe the mechanic fried some wires while trying to fix the temp gauge.Also your alternator mighta been bad and isnt charging the battery that could account for poor spark and low pressure!Good Luck if all else fails and you cant get it running you can still sit in you fiero and day dream of all the fun your missing!!!
Z||000008||FieroMike||08-02-99||01:49 PM||||To re-iterate what Ogre was saying, definately check the small black wire off the negative terminal that terminates at the base of the battery, and replace both the red and black wiring.  Same thing happened to my 6 cylinder and sure enough that's what it was.  
Z||000009||Rod101||08-02-99||07:10 PM||||FYI update:<p>Just came back from the workshop. The technician is not working today so I talked to the desk clerk. (The evil price puncher and so called customer service guy). The latest is that the spark plugs appear to working. Fixed something to do with the wiring (Must be his fault right? ;)). Now he says 'maybe' the fuel pump need to be replaced. Hmmm.. I am not too sure about that. I think I will have to ask the technician about the fuel pump pressure. BTW the pressure should be about 9-13 psi right?<p>Will hang on a little bit longer... must ...not ... give... up...
Z||000010||DJRice||08-03-99||06:51 AM||||If its a 4 banger then 9-13 PSI sounds about right.  If you had a 6 cyl then that would be way low, but I imagine youll be driving that Fiero before too long.<br>
Z||000011||Rod101||08-03-99||09:59 PM||||Update:<p>Some progress is done! It appears that it is the wiring after all. The ORGE is right (HE IS THE MAN). The mechanic has comfirmed that there is nothing wrong with the fuel pump but the wiring from the pump to the fuel relay has something wrong. He rewires it and it starts.<p>Also he says that there is something wrong with the coils at the ignition unit. Appearently the previous owner has changed just ONE of the TWO coil and it results in an 'unbalanced' resistance with one of them (I have no idea what does that mean). Thus making the car difficult to start. He will replace both the coils.<p>Tomorrow he will be doing a cold start test and I am keeping my finger crossed. :)
Z||000012||Rod101||08-05-99||12:40 AM||||She is Alive!... Alive!!!!<p>Yep. All is done. Joy. The car is now working (at least for now). The last thing that needs to work on in the motor mount. It seems that one of them is broken. Have to wait for a few days for the part to come in but in the mean time, I will have to test run and put on some miles on the car to make sure that it works.<p>Many thanks to all who helped and good luck to those who will purchase a Fiero. 
